From 5e5f34d0f654eff1dc4aae11de4a63b13da256c5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dispatch Sandbox <jax1313@outlook.com>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 19:00:18 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 03/12] Replace the dashboard's per-load queries with two
 rollups

get_financial_dashboard() and get_chart_data() each walked every load and asked
the database twice about it -- get_rate_confirmation(load_id) and
list_expenses(load_id). Every one opened its own connection, and SQLite reads
the whole schema into memory before the first statement on a new connection, so
the cost was roughly 1.3 ms per load on a page that loads every load there has
ever been.

Measured through the real portal client, before:

    50 loads    261 ms        500 loads  2,005 ms      2,000 loads  7,786 ms

After:

    50 loads     10 ms        500 loads     18 ms      2,000 loads     47 ms
                                                       5,000 loads    102 ms

The arithmetic is unchanged. Per-load revenue and per-load expenses are still
rounded to cents before they are accumulated -- summing raw floats and rounding
once made this report and store.get_load_profitability_data() disagree by
pennies on identical data, and that convention is the fix for it. So the rollup
returns one row per load and leaves the rounding in the service layer rather
than pushing SUM() into SQL and quietly changing the totals. The rate-confirmation
join picks MIN(rowid) per load, reproducing what fetchone() returned, including
for a load that somehow carries two.

tests/test_dashboard_scaling.py asserts connection *counts*, not milliseconds: a
wall-clock assertion on a shared runner tests the runner. The invariant is that
the count does not grow with the size of the loads table.
